WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.382
Our classroom example begins with a CSV, but real RAG projects usually start with other data sources.

00:00:08.382 --> 00:00:18.697
A company might have PDFs in SharePoint, Word documents in a shared drive, web pages, database records, support tickets,

00:00:18.697 --> 00:00:20.187
object storage, or internal APIs.

00:00:20.187 --> 00:00:29.392
Before RAG can search those sources, the content has to be extracted and normalized.

00:00:29.392 --> 00:00:34.377
For PDFs, that may mean a parser such as PyMuPDF.

00:00:34.377 --> 00:00:37.602
Scanned documents may require OCR.

00:00:37.602 --> 00:00:38.202
Web pages need HTML cleanup.

00:00:38.202 --> 00:00:45.661
A database may already have structured metadata that should be preserved.

00:00:46.111 --> 00:00:51.853
The important point is that this ingestion layer comes before chunking and embeddings.

00:00:51.853 --> 00:01:00.978
Once the text and metadata have been extracted, the rest of the workflow is the same as our classroom example:

00:01:00.978 --> 00:01:08.063
clean the text, create chunks, embed them, store the vectors, and retrieve them when a user asks a question.

00:01:08.063 --> 00:01:14.339
So the CSV is a simplification of the ingestion stage, not a different architecture.
